1. POLICY AS CODE
-----------------
RBAC decides WHO can create a Deployment. It can't say WHAT the
Deployment must look like. Admission policies can:
  - every pod must have resource limits
  - images only from our registry; no ":latest"
  - every namespace gets a default-deny NetworkPolicy
  - add team labels automatically
They run as admission webhooks (Day 36 pipeline) and can VALIDATE
(reject/audit), MUTATE (change), GENERATE (create other objects), and
verify image signatures.


2. THE OPTIONS
--------------
  Kyverno          policies written in YAML (Kubernetes-native),
                   validate/mutate/generate/verifyImages, policy reports
  OPA Gatekeeper   policies in Rego (a policy language), ConstraintTemplate
                   + Constraint CRDs, very flexible
  ValidatingAdmissionPolicy   BUILT INTO Kubernetes (GA in 1.30), rules
                   written in CEL; no extra components. (Mutating
                   admission policies are arriving in newer versions.)
  Pod Security Admission      built-in, fixed profiles (Day 34)


3. A KYVERNO POLICY
-------------------
  apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
  kind: ClusterPolicy
  metadata:
    name: require-team-label
  spec:
    background: true            # also report on existing resources
    rules:
    - name: check-team
      match:
        any:
        - resources:
            kinds: [Deployment]
      validate:
        failureAction: Enforce  # or Audit (report only)
        message: "label 'team' is required"
        pattern:
          metadata:
            labels:
              team: "?*"
(Older Kyverno versions set validationFailureAction at spec level.)

Roll out new policies in Audit mode first, check PolicyReports, then
switch to Enforce.


